\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Fourier / harmonic representations of closed surfaces, shown at three accumulation stages (top: base term; middle: a few terms; bottom: full low-order reconstruction). \emph  {Left column (genus\nonbreakingspace 0):} a star-shaped closed surface parametrised on the sphere with radial function $r(\theta ,\varphi )=\DOTSI \sumop \slimits@ _{\ell ,m}a_{\ell m}Y_{\ell m}(\theta ,\varphi )$; the $Y_{0}^{0}$ term alone is a sphere, and each subsequent spherical harmonic adds a lower-amplitude, higher-frequency deformation. \emph  {Right column (genus\nonbreakingspace 1):} a torus parametrised on $T^{2}=S^{1}\!\times \!S^{1}$ with coordinate map $\mathbf  {X}(u,v)=\DOTSI \sumop \slimits@ _{k,l}\mathbf  {c}_{kl}\,e^{i(ku+lv)}$; the base mode is a standard torus and each additional two-variable Fourier term bends, ripples, or twists it while preserving periodicity in both $u$ and $v$. The two columns illustrate the governing principle of this section: the genus of the surface selects the harmonic basis.}}{4}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:fourier-closed}{{1}{4}{Fourier / harmonic representations of closed surfaces, shown at three accumulation stages (top: base term; middle: a few terms; bottom: full low-order reconstruction). \emph {Left column (genus~0):} a star-shaped closed surface parametrised on the sphere with radial function $r(\theta ,\varphi )=\sum _{\ell ,m}a_{\ell m}Y_{\ell m}(\theta ,\varphi )$; the $Y_{0}^{0}$ term alone is a sphere, and each subsequent spherical harmonic adds a lower-amplitude, higher-frequency deformation. \emph {Right column (genus~1):} a torus parametrised on $T^{2}=S^{1}\!\times \!S^{1}$ with coordinate map $\mathbf {X}(u,v)=\sum _{k,l}\mathbf {c}_{kl}\,e^{i(ku+lv)}$; the base mode is a standard torus and each additional two-variable Fourier term bends, ripples, or twists it while preserving periodicity in both $u$ and $v$. The two columns illustrate the governing principle of this section: the genus of the surface selects the harmonic basis}{figure.caption.13}{}}
